<article class="env-proposition" id="00ZE">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00ZE">Proposition <span data-tag="00ZE">3.3.6.4</span>.</a> The functor $X \mapsto \operatorname{Ex}^{\infty }(X)$ preserves filtered colimits and finite limits. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      It will suffice to show that, for every nonnegative integer $n$, the functor $X \mapsto \operatorname{Ex}^{n}(X)$ preserves filtered colimits and finite limits. Proceeding by induction on $n$, we can reduce to the case $n = 1$. We now observe that the functor $\operatorname{Ex}$ preserves <em>all</em> limits of simplicial sets (either by construction, or because it admits a left adjoint $X \mapsto \operatorname{Sd}(X)$), and preserves filtered colimits by virtue of Remark <a data-tag="00XQ" href="/tag/00XQ">3.3.2.7</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
